Let $g:(0,\infty)\to[0,\infty)$ be smooth and completely monotone, meaning that $(-1)^r g^{(r)}(t)\ge0$ for every integer $r\ge0$ and every $t>0$. For every locally finite configuration $\mathcal C\subset\mathbb R^2$ of centered disk density one, the following equality and inequality hold in the extended nonnegative reals: $$\label{eq:main-inequality}
 E_g(\mathcal C)\ge
 \sum_{a\in A\setminus\{0\}}g(\left\lvert a\right\rvert^2)=E_g(A).$$

For every finite real $k\ge2.36$, put $T_k(s)=k^{-1}e^{-k(s-1)}$. There are entire functions $H_1,H_2$, real on the real axis, and a real radial Schwartz function $f$ on $\mathbb R^2$ such that $$f(x)=e^{-\pi h b\left\lvert x\right\rvert^2}H_1(b\left\lvert x\right\rvert^2),\qquad
 \widehat f(\xi)=e^{-\pi h b\left\lvert\xi\right\rvert^2}H_2(b\left\lvert\xi\right\rvert^2).$$ For every real $s\ge0$, $$\label{eq:normalized-signs}
 H_1(s)\le T_k(s),\qquad H_2(s)\ge0.$$ At every $n\in\mathcal N$, the values and first derivatives satisfy $$\label{eq:normalized-jets}
 \begin{gathered}
 H_1(n)=T_k(n),\qquad H_1'(n)=T_k'(n)=-e^{-k(n-1)},\\
 H_2(n)=H_2'(n)=0.
 \end{gathered}$$

The function in <a href="#eq:cardinal-function" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:cardinal-function">[eq:cardinal-function]</a> extends to an entire function, real on the real axis, with jets $$\label{eq:cardinal-jets}
 p_a(n)=Q_nc_n,\qquad p_a'(n)=Q_n(d_n+D_nc_n)
       \quad(n\in\mathcal N_0).$$ There is a finite complex measure $\mu_a$ on $[-1,1]$ such that $$\label{eq:full-measure}
 p_a(s)=\int_{-1}^{1}e^{i\pi ts}\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm d\mu_a(t)\qquad(s\in\mathbb C),$$ $\mu_a(-E)=\overline{\mu_a(E)}$ for every Borel set $E$, and $$\label{eq:spectral-variation}
 \|\mu_a\|_{\mathrm{TV}}
 \le 25|C|+36\sum_{n\in\mathcal N_0}(|c_n|+|d_n|).$$ For real $s$, the same value can be evaluated from a folded measure on $[0,1]$: $$\label{eq:folded-evaluation}
 p_a(s)=\mathop{\mathrm{Re}}\int_0^1e^{i\pi ts}\,\mathop{}\!\mathrm d\nu_a(t).$$ The atoms of $\nu_a$ are $Ce_jP_j$ at $t_j$, where $e_0=1$ and $e_j=2$ for $1\le j\le6$. Its density on the open frequency intervals is $$\label{eq:folded-density}
 d_j(t)=2\pi\sum_{n\in\mathcal N_0}e^{-i\pi tn}
       \sum_{l=j}^{6}
           \bigl[-\pi c_n(t_l-t)+id_n\bigr]P_le^{i\pi t_ln}
 \quad(t_{j-1}<t<t_j).$$ The same variation bound holds for $\nu_a$.

For every coefficient list $a$, the radial functions $e^{-\pi hs}p_a(s)$ and $e^{-\pi hs}K_{p_a}(s)$ are real Schwartz functions on $\mathbb R^2$ and are Fourier transforms of one another. Consequently, for any two lists, the entire functions $$\label{eq:coupled-pair}
 H_1=p_1+K_{p_2},\qquad H_2=p_2+K_{p_1}$$ give the real radial Schwartz Fourier pair $$f(x)=e^{-\pi hb|x|^2}H_1(b|x|^2),\qquad
 \widehat f(\xi)=e^{-\pi hb|\xi|^2}H_2(b|\xi|^2).$$

For $0\le t\le1$, $$\label{eq:global-kernel-bounds}
 |\lambda(t)|<3,\qquad |z(t)|\le\frac{44}{15}<3,
 \qquad \pi\mathop{\mathrm{Im}}z(t)>.187.$$ The last quantity exceeds $.70$ when $0\le t\le5/6$. On $5/6\le t\le1$, the sharper bounds are $$\label{eq:last-interval-bounds}
 |\lambda(t)|<1.25,\qquad |z(t)|<1.32,
 \qquad \pi\mathop{\mathrm{Im}}z(t)>.187+2.48(1-t).$$ The operator $S$, with extra input coordinates included if desired, maps coefficient lists boundedly to positive-node $\ell^1$ lists and has norm less than 4000. More precisely, if $$w\ge\sum_{n\in\mathcal N_0}(|c_n|+|d_n|),\qquad c\ge|C|,$$ then for every positive integer $r$, $$\label{eq:tail-bound}
 \sum_{\substack{n\in\mathcal N\\n\ge r}}
       \bigl(|(Sa)_{c,n}|+|(Sa)_{d,n}|\bigr)
 \le U_r(w,c):=
 800(w+c)\frac{e^{-.70r}}{1-e^{-.70}}
 +5.3\left(\frac{2.54w}{r}+2c\right)
                  \frac{e^{-.187r}}{1-e^{-.187}}.$$ For several lists, the same formula bounds the sum of their tail outputs when $w,c$ bound the corresponding sums of input coefficients.

The exact arrays defined in this section satisfy $$\label{eq:finite-norms}
\begin{gathered}
 \left\lVert D^{64}\right\rVert<.004,\qquad \left\lVert V_+\right\rVert+\left\lVert V_-\right\rVert<36,\\
 \sum_{i=1}^2\left\lVert(X_i)_j\right\rVert_1<
 \begin{cases}2.3,&1\le j\le5,\\5.6,&6\le j\le10.\end{cases}
\end{gathered}$$ For every specified half-gap, $i=1,2$, consecutive parameter endpoints $K,J$, and $0\le d\le40$, $$\label{eq:bernstein-certificate}
 [L_{i,d}]_{K,J}
 +\mathbf1_{i=1}\sum_{r=0}^d\omega_{dr}a_r^*>.001.$$ For every box and $i=1,2$, $$\label{eq:tail-certificate}
\begin{split}
 [C_i^*]_{K,J}
 &+\frac{\eta_{K,J}\bigl(\sum_{n\in\mathcal N_{\le88}}d^*_{i,n}\bigr)}{s_0}\\
 &+\sum_{n\in\mathcal N_{\le88}}\left(
 \frac{\eta_{K,J}(u_{i,n})}{(s_0-n)^2}
 +\frac{n\,\eta_{K,J}(d^*_{i,n})}{s_0(s_0-n)}\right)>.0021.
\end{split}$$

Let $f$ be a real even Schwartz function on $\mathbb R^2$ whose Fourier transform is nonnegative. Every locally finite configuration $\mathcal C\subset\mathbb R^2$ with centered disk density one satisfies $$\label{eq:density-lp}
 \liminf_{R\to\infty}\frac1{N_R}
 \sum_{\substack{x,y\in\mathcal C_R\\x\ne y}}f(x-y)
 \ge \widehat f(0)-f(0).$$ The same lower bound holds if $f(x-y)$ is replaced by $\Phi(x-y)$, where $\Phi:\mathbb R^2\setminus\{0\}\to[0,\infty)$ satisfies $\Phi(z)\ge f(z)$.

For every $\alpha>0$, let $G_\alpha(x)=e^{-\pi\alpha|x|^2}$. There is a real radial Schwartz function $f_\alpha$ such that, pointwise, $$\label{eq:gaussian-sharp-pair}
 \begin{gathered}
 f_\alpha\le G_\alpha,\qquad \widehat f_\alpha\ge0,\\
 f_\alpha(a)=G_\alpha(a)\quad(a\in A\setminus\{0\}),\qquad
 \widehat f_\alpha(w)=0\quad(w\in A^*\setminus\{0\}).
 \end{gathered}$$

Let $\mathcal C\subset\mathbb R^2$ be locally finite with centered disk density one. Suppose that, for every $\alpha>0$, $$\liminf_{R\to\infty}\frac1{N_R}
 \sum_{\substack{x,y\in\mathcal C_R\\x\ne y}}e^{-\pi\alpha|x-y|^2}
 \ge\sum_{a\in A\setminus\{0\}}e^{-\pi\alpha|a|^2}.$$ Then every smooth completely monotone $g:(0,\infty)\to[0,\infty)$ satisfies $$\label{eq:full-energy-bound}
 E_g(\mathcal C)\ge\sum_{a\in A\setminus\{0\}}g(|a|^2)$$ in the extended nonnegative reals, with the ordered-pair energy defined in <a href="#eq:energy-definition" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:energy-definition">[eq:energy-definition]</a>.

Let $0\le s<2$, and fix $1<p<2$ for $s=0$, or $1<p<\min(2,2/s,3/(s+1))$ for $s>0$. Let $\mathcal A_1$ be the compatible gradient fields in this fixed $L^p_{\rm loc}$ class, with simple locally finite configurations and unit background. Then the limit $$\mathcal W(E)=\lim_{\eta\downarrow0}
 \left[\limsup_{R\to\infty}R^{-2}Q_\eta(E,R)
                    -\kappa g(\eta)\right]$$ exists in $\mathbb R\cup\{+\infty\}$ and is bounded below uniformly over $\mathcal A_1$. Its infimum $m$ is finite. There are integers $R_j\to\infty$ and simple configurations $\mathcal C_j$, periodic under $2R_j\mathbb Z^2$, with $4R_j^2$ points per period cell, whose canonical periodic values satisfy $$V(\mathcal C_j)\longrightarrow m.$$ The approximating fields may therefore be chosen to be canonical periodic gradients in the same fixed-$p$ class.

There is a horizontally stationary probability law $P$ on compatible simple gradient fields in the fixed-$p$ class, supported on $\mathcal W(E)=m$. For the selected dyadic cutoffs, put $$a_\eta=\mathbb E_P\mathcal W_\eta(E),\qquad
 D_\eta=\mathbb E_P\mu_\eta(Q).$$ Then $a_\eta\to m$ and $0\le D_\eta\le m-a_\eta+2\kappa F_\eta\to0$. For each selected $\eta$, one can choose a realization for which the spatial averages of every selected cutoff energy have finite limits, the density $d_\eta=\lim_{R\to\infty}R^{-2}\mu_\eta(K_R)$ satisfies $d_\eta\le2D_\eta$, and, when $k=1$, for every fixed $\varepsilon>0$, $$\frac1{\varepsilon^4R^2}
 \int_{K_R\times\{|y|>\varepsilon^2R/4\}}w|E|^2\longrightarrow0.$$

Fix the auxiliary cutoff $\eta'$ chosen above and $0<\eta<\eta'$. Let $E$ be a compatible simple gradient field for which $$M_{R,\rho}:=R^{-2}Q_\rho(E,R)$$ has a finite limit for $\rho=\eta,\eta'$. If $k=1$, suppose also that $e_{\varepsilon,R}$ defined in <a href="#eq:normalized-moving-tail" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="eq:normalized-moving-tail">[eq:normalized-moving-tail]</a> tends to zero for every fixed $\varepsilon>0$; set $e_{\varepsilon,R}=0$ if $k=0$. For every $0<\varepsilon<1/8$ and every sufficiently large integer $R$, there are exactly $R^2$ distinct points $\widehat\Lambda\subset K_R$ and a field $\widehat E$ with zero exterior normal flux satisfying $$-\operatorname{div}(w\widehat E)
 =\kappa\left(\sum_{p\in\widehat\Lambda}\delta_{(p,0)}
                 -\mathbf1_{K_R}\delta_{\mathbb R^2}\right).$$ Its truncation has finite weighted energy and obeys $$\begin{aligned}
 R^{-2}\int_{K_R\times\mathbb R^k}w|\widehat E_\eta|^2
 &\le M_{R,\eta}
 +C_{s,\eta'}\varepsilon(1+g(\eta))
                     (1+M_{R,\eta}+M_{R,\eta'})+o_R(1)\\
 &\quad+C_s e_{\varepsilon,R}
                     \varepsilon^{1+\min(2\gamma,0)}.
\end{aligned}$$ Every pair in $\widehat\Lambda$ at distance below $2\eta$ is a retained pair of the original configuration in $K_R$, and every point has distance at least $a_0/2$ from $\partial K_R$. The field $\widehat E$ need not be a gradient. All constants are independent of $R$ and the realization; $R$ is chosen after $\eta',\eta$, the field, and $\varepsilon$.
